    "summary": "Stellar Sugar>Scarlet segues, tight jams, high‑energy set",
    "blurb": "The night erupts with a flawless Sugar Magnolia → Scarlet Begonias transition and a rolling Sugar→Scarlet→Man Smart→Eyes sequence. Jerry’s soaring lead work and Brent’s keyboard fireworks drive the band through powerful renditions of Touch of Grey, Jack Straw, Bird Song and a soaring Sunshine Daydream closer.",
    "review": "July 18, 1989 at Alpine Valley captured one of the Dead’s most electrifying summer outings. The first set opens with the bright optimism of “Touch of Grey” and builds momentum through “Jack Straw,” “New Minglewood Blues,” and an inspired “Bird Song” that segues straight into “The Promised Land.”  The second set is a showcase of seamless transitions – the iconic Sugar Magnolia → Scarlet Begonias flow feels effortless, then rockets through “Man Smart, Woman Smarter” into a soaring “Eyes Of The World” before the rhythm section launches into a crisp Drum‑Space pair and a gorgeous “China Doll.”  Brent Mydland’s keyboard wizardry shines on “Dear Mr. Fantasy,” while Phil Lesh’s melodic bass underpins the entire performance.  The show closes with the crowd‑pleasing “Sunshine Daydream” and an enthusiastic encore of “The Mighty Quinn,” leaving the massive Alpine Valley audience buzzing.  Across all available tapes, the band sounds locked in, making this a must‑listen snapshot of the late‑’80s Dead.",
